Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for industrial field I/O (read/write holding registers)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus response objects use .isError() and .registers[]; pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk use different return conventions (e.g. lists or None 
    │   on failure); minimalmodbus is serial-first; using it for TCP requires additional transport shims or a different library variant; unit/slave id 
    │   parameter naming and position differs across libraries (unit= vs slaveaddress= vs slave=); error handling model differs: pymodbus returns error 
    │   objects, minimalmodbus raises exceptions directly; address/register numbering conventions (0-based vs 1-based) can differ between libraries
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── pymodbus is used here strictly as a TCP Modbus master to read/write holding registers on a boiler controller. pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk both provide 
        TCP master clients with comparable register read/write primitives and could serve the same functional role. minimalmodbus is explicitly called out in 
        the file's own docstring as a substitution target, but it is fundamentally a serial (RS-232/RS-485) library with a different read/write API (e.g. 
        read_register/write_register with different signatures) and error handling model (raises exceptions instead of returning response objects with 
        isError()), so it requires a compatibility layer rather than a drop-in swap.

